## Why This Is Asked
Aims to gauge handling of ambiguous prompts with simple, maintainable logic—crucial for multi-tenant data workflows without heavy ML.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight keyword-based intent detection
- Ambiguity handling with clarifying questions
- Structured 2-step plans and module payloads
- Lightweight testing with sample prompts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo JS prototype for disambiguation harness
function detectIntents(text){
  const intents = {
    time: /(last|recent|past|last month)/i,
    region: /(region|country|geo)/i,
    users: /(users|accounts|people)/i
  };
  const hits = Object.keys(intents).filter(k => intents[k].test(text));
  return hits;
}

function disambiguate(text){
  const intents = detectIntents(text);
  if (intents.length > 1){
    return {ambiguous: true, options: ["summarize by region", "summarize by time"]};
  }
  if (intents.length === 1){
    return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Fetch data", "Summarize"], payload: {module: intents[0]}};
  }
  return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Interpret", "Respond"], payload: {module: 'default'}};
}

console.log(disambiguate("summarize recent activity"));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale the heuristic to handle multilingual prompts?
- What tests would you add to prevent regression when adding new intents?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend routing with governance constraints and to design testable prototypes.

## Key Concepts
- multi-module routing with governance
- signal design: intent, data_sensitivity, governance_policy
- edge-case handling: conflicts, missing role

## Code Example
```javascript
function route(prompt, signals){
  if (signals.governance_policy === 'restrict') return {module:'Data-Governance', payload:{prompt}};
  if (signals.data_sensitivity === 'PII') return {module:'PII-redactor', payload:{prompt}};
  return {module:'SQL-builder', payload:{prompt}};
}
module.exports = route;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit test the routing logic for edge cases like conflicting signals?
- How would you extend the router to support additional governance policies without breaking existing behavior?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design guardrails early in prompt pipelines, focusing on simple heuristics, mitigations, and verifiable safety.

## Key Concepts

- Risk scoring from prompts
- PII and system-prompt leakage detection
- Mitigations: redaction, output limits, guardrails
- Guarded prompts and verification

## Code Example

```javascript
function riskScore(prompt) {
  let score = 0;
  if (/\b(email|e-mail|phone)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 3;
  if (/\b(export|download|CSV|exported)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 2;
  if (prompt.toLowerCase().includes("system prompt")) score += 4;
  return Math.min(score, 10);
}
function mitigate(prompt) {
  return prompt
    .replace(/[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/g, "[REDACTED_EMAIL]")
    .replace(/\b\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{4}\b/g, "[REDACTED_PHONE]");
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant workloads with different policy baselines?
- What tests would you add to validate redactions without affecting legitimate outputs?
